+The NOW material contains three anaphoric candidates in published prose. \enquote{Every has a story} remains on the \href{https://patch.com/california/temecula/attention-detail-monument-refresh-underway-across-all-temecula-city-parks}{\textit{Patch} publication page} (30 July 2025), in a paragraph about the city's parks. \enquote{Every has different blocking mechanics} remains on the \href{https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2024/feb/09/super-bowl-chiefs-49ers-travis-kelce-tony-romo}{\textit{Guardian} page} (9 February 2024). Both were checked on 19 September 2026. The occurrences disprove the earlier report of complete absence; their productivity and status in standard usage remain unsettled.
+
+The third candidate, \enquote{Every has its strengths}, differs from the currently indexed \href{https://www.news24.com/life/motoring/new-models/toyota-launches-new-corolla-in-sa-why-it-took-so-long-for-the-car-to-be-introduced-20200501-2}{\textit{News24} article} (1 May 2020), which has \mention{Each}. Direct opening was blocked, but search returned the article text. The source discrepancy is unresolved; the saved concordance is retained. The original screening outputs and the corrected reading are under \texttt{corpus/exclusion\_tests/}.
+
+The COCA reading also records ellipsis (\enquote{if not every, then almost every male head of household}, \textit{Social History}, 1995), reply fragments, and binomials (\enquote{to be open and generous to all and every}, fiction, 2015). Those constructions don't exhaust the saved NOW evidence. The article treats dependence as the ordinary pattern while acknowledging the anaphoric occurrences; the formal fragment represents that ordinary pattern.
 
